---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Atrinik is one open-source fantasy world with a unified, maintained Classic line and independently released clean-room foundations in development."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About Atrinik</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">One world. Two implementations.</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Atrinik is an open-source cooperative role-playing world. Its maintained
      Classic game can be played today while a new engine and toolchain are
      being built in public.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="two-paths-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two paths through the same world</p>
      <h2 id="two-paths-heading">Playable history and a clean-room future</h2>
      <p>
        The two lines share a project and a setting, but they are separate
        implementations with different licenses and release readiness.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="split-grid">
      <article class="path-card path-card--classic">
        <div class="path-card-topline">
          <span class="path-number">Path I</span>
          <span class="status-tag status-tag--live"
            >Playable and maintained</span
          >
        </div>
        <h3>Classic</h3>
        <p>
          The current playable line is actively maintained in the unified{
            " "
          }<code>atrinik/classic</code>{" "}repository. Its client, server,
          editor, libatrinik, and protocol share one version, release notes
          stream, and coordinated artifact set under GPL-2.0-or-later.
        </p>
        <ul class="path-details">
          <li>Current playable implementation</li>
          <li>One repository and supported 5.x release line</li>
          <li>GPL-2.0-or-later software</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card-actions">
          <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic">
            Explore Atrinik Classic on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="path-card path-card--future">
        <div class="path-card-topline">
          <span class="path-number">Path II</span>
          <span class="status-tag">In development</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Next generation</h3>
        <p>
          The replacement is a set of independently released, clean-room,
          MIT-licensed Go and Rust foundations. They are developed in public,
          but do not yet form a complete public download or replace the playable
          Classic line.
        </p>
        <ul class="path-details">
          <li>Clean-room Go and Rust implementation</li>
          <li>MIT-licensed software</li>
          <li>Not yet available as a complete game</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card-actions">
          <a
            class="link-arrow"
            href="https://github.com/atrinik/atrinik/issues/168"
          >
            Follow the replacement roadmap <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="open-development-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Made in the open</p>
      <h2 id="open-development-heading">A world cared for in public</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Release ownership</h3>
        <p>
          Classic plans, source, issues, and releases share one repository and
          coordinated release train. The replacement foundations remain
          independently released, with each repository owning its code, license,
          release information, and technical documentation. Progress is measured
          by completed work and published releases, not launch promises.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Authored world</h3>
        <p>
          Maps, archetypes, stories, graphics, audio, and other authored content
          have mixed terms. Their licenses and attribution are handled per file
          or collection rather than inherited from either engine.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Service foundations</h3>
        <p>
          Signed publishing, protected rendezvous, and a static server directory
          have been implemented as foundations. Rollout and canonical public
          endpoints are still pending, so they are not advertised as live
          services.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="follow-heading">
    <aside class="callout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Follow the journey</p>
        <h2 id="follow-heading">See what is happening now</h2>
        <p>
          Visit the project repositories for current plans, source, and ways to
          contribute.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-actions">
        <a
          class="button button--primary"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/atrinik/issues/168"
          >Read the roadmap</a
        >
        <a class="button button--ghost" href="https://github.com/atrinik/"
          >Browse Atrinik on GitHub</a
        >
      </div>
    </aside>
  </section>
</Base>
